data.content:
Use EF Core 7+'s **`ExecuteUpdateAsync`** and **`ExecuteDeleteAsync`** — they translate directly to SQL `UPDATE`/`DELETE` statements without loading entities into memory:

```csharp
// Operation 1: Bulk update — single UPDATE statement
await _db.Products
    .Where(p => p.LastSoldDate < DateTime.UtcNow.AddMonths(-6))
    .ExecuteUpdateAsync(s => s.SetProperty(p => p.IsActive, false));

// Operation 2: Bulk delete — single DELETE statement
await _db.AuditLogs
    .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < DateTime.UtcNow.AddYears(-1))
    .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
```

**Why this is fast:**
- Generates a single SQL statement (`UPDATE ... SET ... WHERE ...` / `DELETE FROM ... WHERE ...`)
- Zero entities loaded into memory — no tracking, no change detection
- No `SaveChangesAsync()` needed — executes immediately

**Caveats:**
- Bypasses change tracker — any already-loaded entities won't reflect the change
- Bypasses `SaveChanges` interceptors/events — if you have audit logic there, handle it separately
- For very large deletes (2M rows), consider batching to avoid long-running transactions and lock escalation:

```csharp
int deleted;
do
{
    deleted = await _db.AuditLogs
        .Where(l => l.CreatedAt < DateTime.UtcNow.AddYears(-1))
        .Take(10_000)
        .ExecuteDeleteAsync();
} while (deleted > 0);
```

This turns your O(n) memory + 2 round-trips-per-entity into a single server-side operation.
